WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.510
A common C++ range is described by a beginning position and an ending boundary.

2
00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:07.732
The beginning points to the first element if one exists.

3
00:00:07.732 --> 00:00:10.632
The end is usually a past-the-end iterator.

4
00:00:10.632 --> 00:00:17.075
That end value is useful for comparison, but it does not represent an element that code may read or write.

5
00:00:17.075 --> 00:00:19.008
This distinction matters in every loop.

6
00:00:19.008 --> 00:00:23.841
Code may compare an iterator with the end boundary to decide whether traversal should continue.

7
00:00:23.841 --> 00:00:29.318
Code may increment an iterator only when that operation is valid for the range and iterator category.

8
00:00:29.318 --> 00:00:33.828
Code may dereference an iterator only when it refers to a real current element.

9
00:00:33.828 --> 00:00:37.050
Dereferencing the past-the-end marker is a correctness defect.

10
00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:40.271
Iterator pairs should come from the same container or range.

11
00:00:40.271 --> 00:00:46.070
A begin iterator from one vector and an end iterator from another do not describe a valid range.

12
00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:53.158
The same principle applies to custom ranges and standard algorithms: the boundaries must belong together, and the code should make that relationship

13
00:00:53.158 --> 00:00:54.447
clear enough for review.

14
00:00:54.447 --> 00:01:00.568
Modern C++ also supports sentinel-based ranges where the end marker may have a different type from the iterator.

15
00:01:00.568 --> 00:01:04.434
This is common in range-oriented designs and can support flexible traversal.

16
00:01:04.434 --> 00:01:06.367
The safety model remains the same.

17
00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:11.200
Know where traversal starts, know how it stops, and know exactly when dereference is valid.

18
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:12.811
Clear boundaries improve defensive engineering.

19
00:01:12.811 --> 00:01:20.543
When code names the range, keeps begin and end values near each other, and avoids hidden boundary changes, reviewers can reason about the loop

20
00:01:20.543 --> 00:01:23.120
without reconstructing the container state from scattered clues.
